export const SKILL_SETUP = "# /tokenscope:setup — Connect & Provision Emitting\n\nConnect Claude Code to TokenScope and turn on token attribution for this device.\nOne OAuth consent both **authenticates** you (so the read/tag tools work) and\n**provisions emitting** (so your sessions attribute spend). The durable emit\ncredential never passes through this conversation — a local helper redeems it\nprocess-to-process.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- First time using Claude Code with TokenScope on this device.\n- Your sessions stopped emitting and you want to re-provision.\n- After re-installing the plugin or moving to a new host.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Step 1: Ensure the MCP connection is authenticated\n\nConfirm auth by calling the `my_usage` tool. If it returns data (or an\nempty-but-valid usage summary), auth is good. If it reports \"Not authenticated\",\nlet the client complete the browser OAuth consent and retry.\n\n### Step 2: Read any existing device id (idempotency)\n\nGet this host's current `tokenscope.instance_id` if it has one, so re-running\nrotates the existing credential instead of minting a new one. Ask the plugin's\ndevice-identity helper — it prints only non-secret fields:\n\n```bash\nnode \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/device-id.mjs\" --tool claude-code\n```\n\nIt prints `{\"enrolled\":…,\"tool\":…,\"instance_id\":…,\"bearer_host\":…,\"reason\":…}`.\nUse `instance_id` only when `enrolled` is `true`; anything else (including\n`enrolled: false`) → treat this as a fresh device and omit the id.\n\n**Do not open the device settings or credential files to find the id yourself.**\nThey also hold this device's durable emit credential, and reading one would copy\nthat secret into this conversation. The helper exists so you never have to.\n\n### Step 3: Provision emitting\n\nCall the `provision_emit` tool, passing the existing `instance_id` from Step 2 if\nyou found one (omit it for a fresh device). It does **not** return the durable emit\nsecret. It returns:\n\n- a short-TTL (~5 minute) one-time **handoff code**, and\n- a per-instance **redeem URL** plus a short local-redeem instruction.\n\n### Step 4: Redeem locally (process → process, NOT through this chat)\n\nRun the local redeem helper, passing **only the handoff code** `provision_emit`\nreturned — never a redeem URL or an API base:\n\n```bash\nnode \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/claude-redeem.mjs\" --handoff-code <code>\n```\n\nIt redeems the handoff code and writes this device's Claude Code settings\nitself. **Do not** ask for, print, or store the durable credential in this\nconversation, and do not read the settings back to check it.\n\n### Step 5: Confirm\n\nCall `my_usage` again to confirm the round-trip. Then tell the user:\n\n- **Connected** — the read/tag tools are authorised for your TokenScope account.\n- **Emitting provisioned** — this device's Claude Code settings now carry the\n  OTel plumbing.\n- **Restart `claude`** — telemetry config is read at startup, so relaunch for\n  emission to begin.\n- **Next:** tag each repo with `/plugin:tokenscope:tokenscope:project` (writes a\n  `.tokenscope` file) so its sessions attribute to a budget instead of landing as\n  untagged spend.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my_usage` says \"Not authenticated\" | Let the MCP client finish the browser OAuth consent, then retry. |\n| `provision_emit` handoff expired before redeem | Handoff codes are ~5 min single-use — re-run `provision_emit` for a fresh one. |\n| Redeem helper reports a network error (not a 401) | The local helper couldn't reach the server; check the plugin's API base / connectivity. |\n| Sessions still not emitting after redeem | Restart `claude` — the OTel env is read once at process start. |\n"
export const SKILL_TAG = "# /tokenscope-tag — Tag a Session to a Budget and/or Activity\n\nCategorise one of your Claude conversations: assign it to a TokenScope **budget**\n(a project) and/or set an **activity** label. Tagging is categorisation, not\nbudget allocation (which is admin-only).\n\n## When to Use\n\n- A conversation's spend is sitting unallocated and you want it on a budget.\n- You want to label a session by what kind of work it was (the activity).\n- You want to move a session off a budget (back to unallocated).\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Step 1: Fetch budgets and activities\n\nCall **`list_my_projects`** — the budgets you are a current member of and can\nattribute spend to. Each has an `id`, `code`, and `display_name`.\n\nCall **`list_activity_types`** — activity suggestions for your region, your own\npreviously-used tags first. Suggestions only, not an allow-list; the user may type\na free-form activity.\n\n### Step 2: Present and let the user choose\n\nShow the budgets as a short numbered list (code + display name) and the activity\nsuggestions as a second short list. Ask the user to pick:\n\n- a **budget** to assign the session to (or \"off budget\" / \"unallocated\" to clear\n  it), and/or\n- an **activity** label (a suggestion, their own, or \"clear\" to remove it).\n\nThe user may set just one; an unmentioned field is left unchanged.\n\n### Step 3: Tag\n\nDetermine the session id:\n\n- If a session id was provided as the skill argument, use it.\n- Otherwise ask which conversation to tag (the `claude_session_id`, as shown by\n  `my_usage` or in the dashboard). Default to the current conversation only if the\n  user confirms.\n\nCall **`tag_session`** with:\n\n- `session_id` — the conversation id.\n- `project_id` — the chosen budget's `id`; pass `null` to move it off budget;\n  omit to leave the budget unchanged.\n- `activity` — the chosen label; pass `null` to clear it; omit to leave it\n  unchanged.\n\n`tag_session` requires the `tokenscope.tag` scope. If it reports an\ninsufficient-scope error, tell the user their credential is read-only and they\nshould re-run setup to grant tagging.\n\n### Step 4: Confirm\n\nReport what changed: the session id, the budget it now belongs to (or\n\"unallocated\"), and the activity label (or \"cleared\"). If the tool returned an\nownership (403), membership, or ended-budget error, surface its message verbatim\n— it is already user-safe — and do not retry blindly.\n\n## Notes\n\n- You can only tag conversations that are **yours** and budgets you are a\n  **member** of — the server enforces both.\n- Ended budgets reject new tags; pick an active one.\n"
export const SKILL_PROJECT = "# /tokenscope-project — Bind This Repo to a Budget\n\nTag the current repository to a TokenScope **project budget** by writing a\ncommitted `.tokenscope` file at the repo root. Once a repo carries a `.tokenscope`\nfile, sessions run inside it attribute to that budget instead of landing as\nuntagged spend.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Setting up a repo so everyone's sessions in it attribute to the right budget.\n- Re-pointing a repo to a different budget.\n- You don't know the exact project code and want to pick from a list.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Step 1: List your budgets\n\nCall **`list_my_projects`** — the budgets you are a current member of. Each has an\n`id`, `code`, and `display_name`. The value written to `.tokenscope` is the project\n**`code`** (the stable slug), not the UUID.\n\n### Step 2: Present and let the user pick\n\nShow the budgets as a short numbered list (code + display name). Ask which budget\nthis repository's work should bill to.\n\nIf a project code or search text was passed as the skill argument, match it against\nthe listed budgets first (by `code` or `display_name`) and confirm the match with\nthe user rather than guessing.\n\n### Step 3 (optional): Confirm the code resolves\n\nBefore writing, you may call **`resolve_repo_project`** with the chosen `code` to\nconfirm it maps to a budget you are a member of and echo back its `display_name`\nand `type`. If it reports the code is not a member project, do not write the file;\nre-pick from `list_my_projects`. `resolve_repo_project` resolves by project\n**code** or **code_hash** only — never your repo path or working directory.\n\n### Step 4: Write `.tokenscope`\n\nWrite a file named exactly `.tokenscope` at the **repository root** with the chosen\nproject code as its sole content — plain text, one line, e.g.:\n\n```\nTokenScope-MVP\n```\n\nUse your own file-writing tool — the MCP server never writes the user's disk. If a\n`.tokenscope` already exists with a different code, show the user the old and new\nvalues and confirm before overwriting.\n\n### Step 5: Confirm and advise committing\n\nTell the user:\n\n- The repo is now bound to budget **{code}** ({display_name}).\n- **Commit `.tokenscope`** so teammates in this repo attribute to the same budget.\n  It contains only a non-secret project code — safe to commit.\n- **Restart `claude`** if it was already running in this repo, so the new tag is\n  picked up.\n"
export const SKILL_USAGE = "# /tokenscope-usage — Show Your Month-to-Date Usage\n\nShow your current-month TokenScope usage, split per budget, plus unallocated and\ntagged (off-budget, categorised) spend.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- You want a quick read on where your token spend went this month.\n- You want to see how much is sitting unallocated (and needs tagging).\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Step 1: Fetch usage\n\nCall the **`my_usage`** tool. It returns a usage summary with this shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"month_to_date\": \"2026-06\",\n  \"total_cost_usd\": \"760.27\",\n  \"total_tokens\": 1903724896,\n  \"total_allocation_usd\": \"1000.00\",\n  \"base_allowance_usd\": \"100.00\",\n  \"total_quota_usd\": \"1100.00\",\n  \"buckets\": [\n    { \"project_code\": \"TokenScope-MVP\", \"display_name\": \"TokenScope MVP\", \"cost_usd\": \"760.27\", \"tokens\": 1903724896, \"allocation_total_usd\": \"1000.00\", \"is_active_now\": true, \"source\": \"assigned\", \"ended\": false }\n  ],\n  \"unallocated\": {\n    \"total_cost_usd\": \"322.32\", \"tagged_cost_usd\": \"289.69\", \"untagged_cost_usd\": \"23.32\",\n    \"needs_tagging_count\": 8, \"untaggable_cost_usd\": \"9.30\",\n    \"soft_cap_usd\": \"100.00\", \"over_soft_cap\": true\n  },\n  \"tagged_spend\": [\n    { \"activity\": \"Presentations\", \"cost_usd\": \"184.22\", \"tokens\": 460000000, \"sessions\": 3 }\n  ],\n  \"freshness_minutes_ago\": 3,\n  \"note\": \"Updated from attribution_record.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Step 2: Render three sections\n\n**1. Budgeted (project) spend** — a markdown table from `buckets`:\n\n```\n| Project | Cost (USD) | Tokens | % of total |\n|---------|-----------:|-------:|-----------:|\n```\n\nSort by `cost_usd` desc; `% of total` is against `total_cost_usd`. Flag any\nbucket whose `ended` is true as **(ended)** next to its name.\n\n**2. Unallocated** (spend not on a project budget) — from `unallocated`: one line\n`Unallocated: <total_cost_usd> / soft cap <soft_cap_usd>` and flag **OVER** when\n`over_soft_cap` is true; then\n`tagged <tagged_cost_usd> · untagged <untagged_cost_usd> · <needs_tagging_count> need tagging`,\nadding `· no session <untaggable_cost_usd>` when that is above `0.00` — usage the\nproviders reported with no session behind it (Claude Chat, the Copilot coding\nagent, the other non-Code surfaces). It counts toward the total and the soft cap\nlike every other dollar; there is just no session on it to tag, so it is never in\nthe tagging queue and must not be included in any \"needs tagging\" nudge.\nOmit the section if `total_cost_usd` is `0.00`.\n\n**3. Tagged spend** (categorised by activity, off-budget) — a table from\n`tagged_spend`, sorted by cost desc; skip if empty:\n\n```\n| Activity | Cost (USD) | Sessions |\n|----------|-----------:|---------:|\n```\n\n### Step 3: Footer + nudges\n\nFooter line: `Updated <freshness_minutes_ago> min ago · Anthropic actuals lag ~1 hr`.\nIf `needs_tagging_count > 0`, nudge the user to tag those sessions\n(`/tokenscope-tag` for a specific session, `/tokenscope-project` to bind the\nrepo, or in the web app).\n\nIf `buckets` is empty: tell the user \"No attribution data yet — your sessions will\nstart showing once OTel ingestion is live (and the repo is tagged).\"\n\nIf `my_usage` reports an authentication error, the MCP connection isn't\nauthorised — run `/tokenscope-setup` to connect.\n"
